 used  serbo-croatian for four years before  Macedonia became its own country so I  knew a bunch of different languages but  now they're all a mishmash in my head hi  what's the UM dangerous or scariest  situation you're ever in um probably the  scariest situation I was ever in I  described in the book and it was kind of  auxilary to being a spy it could have  happened to me under any circumstances  but I was um I was in Macedonia and I  was riding my bike actually I just  finished putting a chalk mark on a water  fountain so I guess it did have  something to do with being a spy  and at that time by way of background  Macedonia during the years that I was  there was kind of falling apart it had  been one of the few Balkan countries not  to break down into civil war but during  the time I was there it was on the verge  of civil war and three Macedonian  soldiers this kind of motley crew of  Macedonian soldiers mistook me for an  Albanian rebel and jumped into the  bushes and readied their ak-47s as I was  riding by on my bike and luckily I could  speak the language well enough that I  could stop and convince them that I  wasn't a member of the coast of our  liberation party but that was probably  the scariest thing that ever happened I  was just wondering if what you what the  finished project was was this about  what you intended and how much that you  had in there was actually taken out  through the cia's I guess process  process it was a lot longer when I  initially wrote it but most of the cuts  were mandated by my editor at Putnam not  by the CIA the CIA I have to say was  very fair  in my estimation in what they asked me  to take out you know I kind of had a  back-and-forth dialogue with the  publication Review Board  and everything that they asked me to  take out or change I did and there was  nothing that I felt like detracted from  the book the book is you know as I said  it's it's kind of kind of has a cheeky  tone and I had wondered if they would  make me take out parts of the book that  were either directly or indirectly  critical of the agency and they didn't  so I was I was pretty impressed by that  did you know you were going to write a  book and did you take notes or would  that have not been safe to do the  question if you didn't hear it was did  you know you were going to write a book  and did you take notes I didn't know I  was going to write a book I mean I had  always loved writing and one of the many  naivete is I had joining the agency was  this thought that I could be a spy and  in my spare time I would write short  stories or poetry or something like that  and then I quickly realized that a spy  doesn't have spare time but I never  expected to write a book about my  experience at the agency and I didn't  keep any notes and that that in fact  would have been considered a security  violation to have any notes on my person  that that spoke of my experience as a  spy what made you not stay in working  for the CIA and you're retiring what was  a reason um I left the agency for a  number of reasons and and some of them  were personal I I realized after I  became a spy in CIA terminology it's  called a case officer a lot of CIA  people bristle if you call a case  officer a spy but I'm using spy just  because I I think it's what most people  recognize  but I was found myself very  uncomfortable with what the actual  essence of being a spy is you can kind  of tell from the passage that I just  read that what you're doing is you're  you're praying upon foreigners a little  bit and trying to get them to give you  information and I found that I was very  uncomfortable from the get-go with this  notion of trying to get someone to do  something that I would never do which is  to betray my country or commit espionage  and trying to make a case that that was  in their best interests so on the  personal front I just realized that I  actually didn't enjoy being a spy and  didn't want to devote my life to that  and then on a kind of I guess  ideological front  I found the agency to be a very stifling  environment and I had thought that it  would be an environment that would  really foster independent thinking and  creativity and I found it to be just the  opposite and so there were a number of  disillusionment s' along the way  culminating probably with September 11th  which I viewed as the the biggest  intelligence failure certainly of my  generation and the agency sluggishness  in responding to that  what are your thoughts on the current  shake-up going on right now is it is it  going to help the agency you know I  would have been the first one to  advocate change at the agency but I  don't think that it's it's being  undertaken in the most graceful of  manners under Porter Goss I guess and  the few people that I've known who have  been purged ironically I thought they  were some of the best people that the  agency had another disillusionment that  I had with the agency toward the end of  the five years was this sense that the  organization was becoming increasingly  more partisan when I first joined the  agency I can say I give everyone who  worked their credit for really keeping  politics out of their work both on the  operations end and the analysts it tends  to be a kind of hawkish conservative  environment but I never saw anyone let  their personal political beliefs get in  the way of what their mission was  whether it be in operation or as an  analyst producing the best analysis they  could and I think I saw that change  particularly during the build-up to the  Iraq war when it seemed to me that the  agency had kind of been co-opted and was  being used as a tool by the  administration to achieve its agenda of  going to war so that was incredibly  disheartening to me and I think that it  it seems to be going more in that  partisan direction under Porter Goss  where there are many women colleagues  was it an old boys club so to speak it's  still very much an old boys club for  sure but I did have a number of female  colleagues in my training class I can't  say how many people were in my training  class but almost half of us were women  and in my estimation the best spies that  I knew were women the best case officers  that I knew the most successful the most  aggressive and I think the most  professional across the board were women  and  that was you know that was really  interesting for me to discover and in  part the subject of this article that I  wrote for Washingtonian but I think it  comes down to a lot of being a good spy  is relying on your intuition and I think  women are in general naturally very good  at being intuitive and reading people  and that that's 90 percent of the job in  your book you talk a lot about having to  lie to people in your life and I  especially are friends Emin Emily that  you still manage to spend time with and  I was just curious what the fallout for  you personally has been and if you've  had a lot of negative fallout from  people finding out the truth I've had I  guess more positive fallout from people  finding out the truth but I have  realized retrospectively that a lot of  people were angry at me during this this  sort of gap in my life where I wasn't in  touch with with anyone  and since I have you know come out I  guess  the a lot of friends have written to me  and said well I'm glad that I finally  know why we never heard from you  I had the sense when I was actually  going through it that I was losing these  friendships but I guess I didn't realize  how hurt people were by what I did which  was basically go incommunicado for four  five years I would do it again and  people ask me a lot would you recommend  it to anyone else a young man or woman  who was considering going into the  career of spy tum' and I would I really  would because I know for myself and my  parents can probably attest to the fact  that no matter how hard anyone tried to  discourage me from taking this path I  was intent upon doing it realizing that  it wasn't for me was something that only  I could do but I think that's something  if someone has that that itch  that they they have to scratch it  themselves I guess so and also the  agency desperately needs good people you  know I'm not the only one from my  training class who's left a number of us  have left  and I thought they were some of the best  people who ended up leaving in a very  short period of time just like I did and  that's a shame for that organization and  it's a shame for our country right over  here you familiar with the case of from  the Supreme Court now with the Soviet  witness protection and only if you had  any thoughts on that and and how strange  it is to enforce something like that  when you usually make a deal but it's  not really legal in the first place I'm  a little bit familiar with the case  she's referring to and for those of you  who are not I guess there's a case going  before the Supreme Court now where an  Eastern European correct me if I say  anything wrong but an Eastern European  couple who had been foreign agents  working for the CIA are now suing the  agency saying that the agency didn't  fulfill its end of the whatever bargain  they had and the agency is seeking to  quash the entire lawsuit saying that  these people don't even have a right to  bring this to court  I think this couple are American  citizens now because that's the the  nature of espionage or the nature of  spine you can never actually sue the  organization that you're spying for not  knowing a lot about the case you know I  don't know who's in the right or who's  in the wrong but I think there's a  danger of you know it's it's very hard  as a case officer as a CIA case officer  to recruit people under any  circumstances and it's increasingly hard  as America has become less and less  popular throughout the world something  like this makes it even harder and I'm  not blaming the couple or the agency but  a situation like this where if it does  emerge that the agency hasn't fulfilled  its end of the bargain to this couple  who obviously put their lives on the  line for the CIA and for our country it  kind of sends a message worldwide to  foreigners that perhaps it's not in  their best interest to work for us and I  think that that makes the job of every  CIA case officer even more difficult  and you sir have last question there's  two questions for me is that right have  you seen the movie the recruit and how  accurate is that of the farm I just  watched it the other day in fact for the  second time I watched it's a I mean it's  a ludicrously it's a looot kind of  ludicrous premise the whole movie but  the the actual scenes that take place  down at the farm are really accurate and  I think the movie although I would say  kind of a stupid movie really does a  good job of capturing like the mystique  of the farm I watched it with a former  colleague of mine who also had been to  the farm and and we were just amazed you  know that we both kind of got that same  sort of queasy but excited feeling  watching Colin Farrell I mean there's  nobody like Colin Farrell at the CIA for  sure but I'm watching a Colin Farrell  you know ride down on this school bus to  the find it really reminded us of what  we had gone through  no that was my Soviet okay all right  and if that is all the questions we have  we want to thank Lindsay for being here  this evening the book is called blowing  my cover my life as a CIA spy please  help me in thanking Lindsay Moran  Lindsay Moran is author of blowing my  cover my life as a CIA spy it's  published by Putnam on the web at  penguin com
 

